{
  "requirement": "A {term}`requirements.references.definition_selection` in a requirements input MUST continue to select requirements and named examples, excluding named definitions. Definitions referenced by selected texts MUST be captured as separate definition inputs, regardless of whether their names match the selection. Sharing a selection's matching name prefix alone MUST NOT make a definition a dependency.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the sources satisfy `requirements.definitions.selection`.**\n\n**Implementation: selection excludes named definitions**\n- `InputSelector::resolve` (`src/inputs.rs`) handles the `Requirements` form. It skips every `DefinitionKind::Term` and keeps only names matching the glob.\n- A glob or exact name that matches only a named definition yields `matches no requirement or example`. An exact name without `*` therefore selects only that requirement or example.\n- Every other consumer applies the same filter. They are `source_input`, `status_markdown_with_options`, `info_markdown` and the evidence validator (`validate_resolved_input`, which calls `resolve`).\n\n**Implementation: dependencies come from referenced terms only**\n- `terms::direct_references` scans only the bodies of the selected declarations in a `Requirements` input. It uses `definition_blocks` and collects `{term}` references from them.\n- It never derives a dependency from the glob or its name prefix.\n- `close_dependencies` expands those references transitively and requires each one to be a `Term`. A term is therefore captured whether or not its name matches the selection.\n- `add_input::update_inputs` writes each required term as its own `{\"definition\": …}` input, replacing any duplicates.\n- `authoring::definition_inputs` supplies them as mandatory inputs.\n- `validate_dependencies` reports any required term that does not appear exactly once as a definition input.\n\n**Marking**\n- The functions above are marked `{impl}` for this requirement. I found no unmarked code that implements it.\n- Related code that filters out terms, such as the `references` tool, is marked for `definition_globs` and falls outside this requirement's \"requirements input\" scope.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/definitions.rs::globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob`:\n  - `rules.*` matches only `rules.ready`, and the selected text contains no `{definition}` block.\n  - Captured definitions are exactly `glossary.a`, `glossary.b` (transitive) and `rules.used`. `rules.used` shares the glob prefix and is captured because the text references it, as the requirement's \"regardless of whether their names match\" clause demands.\n  - `rules.unrelated` shares the prefix but is not referenced, and is not required. Validation raises no error mentioning it.\n- `tests/definitions.rs::selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions`:\n  - The glob matches `[\"rules.case\",\"rules.ready\"]`, not the definition `rules.used`.\n  - `add_input` with the exact term name fails with `matches no requirement or example`.\n  - The `status` and `info` commands exclude `rules.used`, and `info rules.used` fails.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs::requirements_reads_select_requirements_and_examples_but_not_definitions` covers the authoring tool path. A glob selects the requirement and the example, and an exact term name is rejected.\n\nThe tests cover the main cases: inclusion, exclusion, an unreferenced prefix-sharing term, a referenced prefix-sharing term, transitive dependencies, the exact-name case, and the CLI, add-input and authoring paths. No gaps and no FIXMEs were found.",
  "references": "2f245a0a2a79fa55401b14b2ff61f57c0953d41d08e781ce65ef052194505e72",
